Q: AIoT device: Radius Authentication using PHP with Message-Authenticator attribute A: <p>The Message-Authenticator attribute (type 80) in RADIUS is not natively supported by the PHP radius extension.
It requires calculating an HMAC-MD5 hash of the packet, which is typically handled by third-party PHP RADIUS libraries.</p>
<p>You can use a pure PHP RADIUS client library to do this,
try <a href="https://github.com/BlockBox2000/php-radius" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/BlockBox2000/php-radius</a>.
It can be used for Iot, AIoT, or AI applications, and file size is 32k only.</p>
<p>The one you need is b2radius.inc, and you can modify it.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php
require_once('b2radius.inc');
$radius = new Radius('192.168.11.1', 'testing123');
if ($radius-&gt;AccessRequest('userid', 'userpw')) {
  echo &quot;Authentication accepted.\n&quot;;
} else {
  echo &quot;Authentication rejected.\n&quot;;
}
echo $radius-&gt;GetReadableReceivedAttributes();
?&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: @WebMvcTest is not considering secured endpoints A: <p>The issue occurs because @WebMvcTest is a sliced test that scans for Spring MVC infrastructure but does not automatically pull in your custom @Configuration classes where security rules (like SecurityFilterChain) are defined.</p>
<p>Use the @Import annotation to bring in your SecurityConfig class. This ensures the MockMvc instance respects your actual security rules (roles, CSRF, etc.).</p>
<pre><code>@WebMvcTest(DemoController.class)
@Import(SecurityConfig.class) // Explicitly import your security logic
public class YourControllerTest {
    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = &quot;USER&quot;) // Use this to simulate an authenticated user
    void testSecuredEndpoint() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(get(&quot;/hello&quot;))
               .andExpect(status().isOk());
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Recursion help please A: <p>When solving this problem, you had to pay attention to the fact that in each recursive call the numbers are output twice, which means that these recursive calls should be 2 times less (<code>writeSequence(n - 2</code>).</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public void writeSequence(int n) {
    if (n &lt; 1) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
    var m = (n + 1)/2;
    if (n &gt; 1) {
        System.out.print(m + &quot; &quot;);
        if (n &gt; 2) writeSequence(n - 2);
        System.out.print(m + &quot; &quot;);
    }
    else System.out.print(m + &quot; &quot;);
}
</code></pre>
